  "query_preview": "..Moisture remains in place over much of the forecast\narea today, allowing for another day of scattered showers and\nthunderstorms across central and southern Utah with more isolated\ncoverage across northern Utah. As of 150PM, shower and\nthunderstorm activity is already well underway for central central\nUtah, as well as a portion of northern Utah. This activity is\nexpected to continue through the remainder of the heating hours\nacross the region. That said, it will be difficult for storms to\nsustain themselves as they drift off terrain features due to a\nlack of upper level support.\n\nAn area of drier air is expected to work its way into the forecast\narea tomorrow which will act to decrease the overall potential for\nconvection during the heating hours. As such, coverage of showers\nand thunderstorms are expected to be much more isolated on Tuesday\nafternoon, particularly limited to the high terrain in\nnortheastern and a portion of central Utah. Due to decreasing PWAT\nvalues and lack of lower level moisture, the overall threat for\nheavy rainfall and flash flooding will be quite low, but not\nimpossible, tomorrow afternoon. Moisture is anticipated to surge\nnorthward once again on Wednesday, bringing one more round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms capable of producing heavy\nrainfall and flash flooding across the eastern half of Utah.\n\nFrom Thursday forward, the area of high pressure (the center of\nwhich is currently just east of the Four Corners region) will\nshift south and west while an offshore trough remains parked over\nthe PacNW region, helping to introduce a drier air mass into the\nregion. As such, any chance for showers and thunderstorms will be\ngreatly reduced as we finally rid the forecast area of monsoonal\nmoisture. Contrarily, we'll begin to see some of the driest\nconditions since the monsoonal moisture arrived over the area in\nmid-July as afternoon humidity falls back into the single-digits\nfor many low lying areas. Pairing with the very dry conditions,\nafternoon high temperatures are expected to rise back into the\nlow- to mid-100s on the Wasatch Front, with lower Washington\ncounty potentially rising into the low-110s. Overnight low\ntemperatures are also expected to remain elevated during this time\nwhich will limit the amount of relief from the daytime heat. Risk\nfor heat related illness is likely to increase again through this\nperiod (Thursday through Sunday).",
